Subject: [PATCH] devtools: hide an attached CDP client from page script

Stack capture for exceptions and console calls follows Debugger.enable,
the domain DevTools uses and Playwright and Puppeteer skip, so a client
that sends only Runtime.enable does not change what those calls cost a
page. Console serialization of errors reads only own properties, so a
getter on Error.prototype cannot count the reads. The
--remote-debugging-port endpoint closes requests that other pages send,
identified by Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site headers that script cannot set,
so a page cannot probe the port while drivers connect as usual. V8's
heap limit follows the profile's memory, clamped to the host, so
performance.memory agrees with navigator.deviceMemory.

--- a/content/browser/devtools/devtools_http_handler.cc
+++ b/content/browser/devtools/devtools_http_handler.cc
@@ -126,6 +126,65 @@
   return url.HostIsIPAddress() || net::IsLocalHostname(url.GetHost());
 }
 
+// Apostate: true when this request was issued by a web page rather than by a
+// DevTools client.
+//
+// A page cannot reach the protocol itself - there is no CORS header here and
+// the WebSocket paths carry unguessable ids - but it does not need to. It only
+// needs to learn that something answered, and `fetch(url, {mode: 'no-cors'})`
+// resolving rather than rejecting tells it that. Measured on this build from a
+// document in the local address space: the fetch resolves with
+// --remote-debugging-port and rejects with TypeError without it, four times out
+// of four, in well under a millisecond. That is a boolean "this browser is
+// being automated over TCP", and it is the default transport for Puppeteer.
+// Local Network Access does block the same probe from a document in the public
+// address space at this revision, so the surviving reach is documents in the
+// local and private address spaces, plus any configuration where those checks
+// are off.
+//
+// The discriminators are headers the network stack adds on the document's
+// behalf. Both are forbidden header names, so page script can neither set nor
+// clear them.
+//
+// Origin comes first because it is the one a WebSocket handshake carries; the
+// handshake has no Fetch Metadata at all. A page's origin is always http,
+// https, or the opaque "null" a sandboxed frame sends. So a non-http scheme is
+// not a page - that is the chrome:// and devtools:// front-ends, including the
+// one chrome://inspect opens against a remote target - and the single http
+// origin that belongs here is this endpoint's own, which is what the front-end
+// it serves sends back.
+//
+// Sec-Fetch-Site covers the rest: it is present on every subresource fetch,
+// image load, script load and frame navigation, confirmed against a capture of
+// this build's own requests. Its value is `none` only for a navigation the user
+// started, which is how a human reaches the discovery page, and `same-origin`
+// only for the bundled front-end loading its own files. Everything a page can
+// cause is `same-site` or `cross-site`.
+//
+// Clients that are not browsers - curl, and the Node HTTP and WebSocket stacks
+// Puppeteer and Playwright are built on - send neither header and are
+// unaffected. A client that does send Origin, as the Python websocket-client
+// library does, sends the endpoint's own and is unaffected too.
+bool RequestIsFromWebPage(const net::HttpServerRequestInfo& info) {
+  const std::string origin = info.GetHeaderValue("origin");
+  if (!origin.empty()) {
+    if (origin == "null")
+      return true;
+    const GURL origin_url(origin);
+    if (!origin_url.is_valid() || !origin_url.SchemeIsHTTPOrHTTPS())
+      return false;
+    // Compare against the authority the request was addressed to rather than a
+    // fixed port, because the port is chosen at launch.
+    const std::string host = info.GetHeaderValue("host");
+    return host.empty() || origin_url != GURL("http://" + host);
+  }
+
+  const std::string fetch_site = info.GetHeaderValue("sec-fetch-site");
+  if (fetch_site.empty())
+    return false;
+  return fetch_site != "none" && fetch_site != "same-origin";
+}
